Quoting  Jakob Creutzig  <creutzig@mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de>:
>David Damerell <damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk> writes:
>>It could be a deathtrap for someone who hasn't prepared themselves
>>sensibly for such a situation. That's how the game works. I don't see a
>>problem here.
>Magic traps could impose a soft cap onto how far one can push this.   
>It happened to me that until midgame I hadn't a decent method of 
>escape.

Midgame, though, you generally don't have a million alchemised hitpoints.
I don't think harder hitting endgame monsters would make a difference to
the existing behaviour of early magic traps (which sometimes, yes, can be
game-over-insert-coin).

>What I was thinking about was another idea, bleeding wounds.  That is, 
>if a monster scores a good hit, you will not only suffer damage this 
>round, but a smaller damage for several rounds.

It's a bit of an Angbandism, but it would be a neat idea as to how to have
monsters inflict higher damage without oneshotting players. A severe bleed
is another ticking timer; you'd need to retreat from combat (so as to take
only bleeding damage) or take some action to fix it. I'd imagine any
healing potion would fix all bleeding, for example.
